{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Happy is the man who fears always, but hardens his heart falls into evil (Proverbs 28:14).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b>, peace be upon him, commands in this verse (Proverbs 28:14) that a person should be soft-hearted and should always fear, in all his affairs and in all his actions, and should look and reflect upon their ultimate end; and he warns him not to harden his heart lest he be punished. He used the expression אשרי ('happy'), and we do not find this word in all of Scripture in the singular but only in the plural. The reason for this is that a person is not pronounced happy and praised for one good trait or one virtue alone, but only when many virtues and good traits are found in him. Thus it is written (Psalms 65:5): ״אשרי תבחר ותקרב ישכון חצריך״ — and the chosen one, the brought-near one, and the one who dwells in the courts of the house of HaShem is not so for one virtue alone but for many together. Likewise (Psalms 84:6): ״אשרי אדם עז לו בך מסלות בלבבם״ — and he explains 'מסלות' as the pathways of the heart in many ascending degrees in the trait of fear and trust. Likewise: ״HaShem צבאות, happy is the man who trusts in You״ — for in the trait of trust many good virtues and many mighty traits of felicity are included. Likewise (Psalms 1:1): ״אשרי האיש אשר לא הלך״ — he goes on enumerating a multiplicity of virtues: did not walk, did not stand, did not sit. So too here: ״אשרי אדם מפחד תמיד״ — he means: whoever fears always in all his actions, and reflects upon them and contemplates their ultimate end in regard to their harm and benefit before he performs them, assuredly has many good commandments through which he is good in the eyes of Elohim and man. And because one who possesses this trait is thereby drawn after the intellect and comes against matter, therefore he said אדם and not איש, for the word אדם is derived from אדמה ('earth'), as if he said: happy is the one derived from earth, hewn from matter, who abandons matter and is drawn toward the intellect — who fears always in all his affairs and all his actions and sets his heart upon their ultimate end, for whoever does so will never sin. Take the matter of eating and drinking: it is not fitting that a person intend in this the sensation of taste and the filling of desire, for in this matter he shares with the beast; rather, he should intend the ultimate end — and what is the ultimate end? That his body be healthy and strong and able to serve the Holy One, blessed be He; and anything other than this ultimate end — that is sin. So too in the matter of conjugal relations: it is not fitting that he intend bodily pleasure, for in this too he shares with the beast; rather, he should intend the ultimate end — and what is the ultimate end? The intention of procreation, for the perpetuation of the species, and to bring forth children who will be servants of the Holy One, blessed be He; and anything other than this intention is sin. So too in the matter of acquiring wealth: it is fitting for him to be satisfied with little, to deal in faithfulness, and that the ultimate intent of his accumulating money be to distribute it in worthy causes and to spend it on things that will be for the well-being of his body and the success of his soul. He should beware not to oppress, not to rob his fellow, not to wrong him, and reflect on the consequence: if he robs or wrongs him and profits his body in this world, he loses his soul in the world to come; and the money he coveted illicitly for a fleeting moment — it is a sin deserving the sentence of death; the punishment will overtake him and slay him in the end. It will befall him like the bird that covets the grain within the snare: its desire causes it to be caught in the net. Of this Solomon the king, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 1:17–18): ״כי חנם מזורה הרשת בעיני כל בעל כנף והם לדמם יארובו יצפנו לנפשותם״ — and he drew this parable against workers of iniquity, coveters of illicit money, who see temporary pleasure for themselves but do not see the ultimate retribution. Of this Solomon, peace be upon him, also said (Proverbs 20:17): ״ערב לאיש לחם שקר ואחר ימלא פיהו חצץ״. So too in all his other actions, one must always fear, lest he come to anything contrary to the Torah — neither in his words nor in his deeds. ״But hardens his heart falls into evil״ — one who does not reflect upon the ultimate end of his actions is the one who hardens his heart, and this is nothing but evil-heartedness and cruelty within him. The soft-hearted one who fears always is the opposite of the one who hardens his heart: the one who hardens his heart has no fear of Elohim before his eyes; he does not see the ultimate reward in commandments nor the ultimate punishment in transgression. But one who fears always sees the retribution before it arrives, for he fears it; while one who hardens his heart does not see it until it comes upon him. The one who fears things continually — this happens to him by way of humility and lowliness, and therefore he is raised up and exalted — this is why the expression אשרי was used, which is a term of ascent. But one who hardens his heart — it comes to him by way of pride and haughtiness of heart, and therefore the expression יפול ('falls') was used. And it says יפול ברעה — meaning, even in a single evil, as it is written (Psalms 34:22): ״תמותת רשע רעה״, and (Proverbs 24:16): ״כי שבע יפול צדיק וקם ורשעים יכשלו ברעה״, and (Proverbs 16:4): ״כל פעל ה' למענהו וגם רשע ליום רעה״ — Scripture teaches you that the wicked person is prepared for punishment and readied for the day of evil, and even his punishment and retribution is למענהו — that is, for the glory of the Holy One, blessed be He, so that the name of Heaven be sanctified, as it is said (Ezekiel 38:23): ״והתגדלתי והתקדשתי ונודעתי לעיני גוים רבים״. Thus we find with Pharaoh the wicked, that he hardened his heart, and therefore he fell and perished in the end. He himself hardened his heart, as it is said (Exodus 9:34): ״ויכבד לבו הוא ועבדיו״, and it is written (Exodus 8:11): ״ויכבד פרעה את לבו״; and the Holy One, blessed be He, hardened his heart yet more, in order to punish him and to multiply His signs and wonders in the land of Egypt — this is what is written." "<b>And HaShem said to Moses: Come to Pharaoh, for I have hardened his heart and the heart of his servants, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: the ten plagues — דצ״ך עד״ש באח״ב — just as they are arranged in the Torah in this order, so were the first two with warning and the third without warning: ד״צ with warning; ך׳ without warning; ע״ד with warning; ש׳ without warning; ב״א with warning; ח׳ without warning. The plague of the firstborn, which is the tenth, was with warning — for it returns to the first order. This is what our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted, and the scriptural passages prove the same." "<b>And if</b> you examine the Scriptures in all the parashot you will find that the first of the two that come with warning says ״התיצב לפני פרעה״ — 'station yourself before Pharaoh' — and the second says ״בא אל פרעה״ — 'come to Pharaoh.' He began with the plague of blood: ״לך אל פרעה בבקר ונצבת לקראתו על שפת היאור״, and with the plague of frogs he mentioned ״בא אל פרעה״. The plague of lice — there is no warning there; from this our Rabbis of blessed memory derived: one who was stricken and repeated, he is not warned again. With the plague of wild beasts he returned to his first order and mentioned there ״השכם בבקר והתיצב לפני פרעה״ — here the scheme emerges. And with the plague of pestilence he mentioned ״בא אל פרעה״. The plague of boils — there is no warning there. With the plague of hail he returned to his first order and mentioned there ״השכם בבקר והתיצב לפני פרעה״, and with the plague of locusts here he mentioned ״בא אל פרעה״. The plague of darkness — there is no warning there." "<b>Therefore</b> it appears to me to explain that Moses would inform him of the plague and would give him warning once at the riverbank and once a second time in his palace. The expression ״בא אל פרעה״ that Scripture mentions each time, its meaning is that he would enter his palace. The Scriptures prove this: from the time he warned him about the plague of blood at the riverbank, Scripture reported that immediately Pharaoh went to his palace, as it says: ״ויבא אל ביתו ולא שת לבו גם לזאת״. The reason all the warnings followed this pattern — one warning at his river and another warning in his palace — was to demonstrate punishment for his pride and his arrogance, for he used to boast about his river, as it is written (Ezekiel 29): ״לי יאורי ואני עשיתיני״, and he used to boast in his palace — and that is what is written ״ויבא אל ביתו״ — meaning that because he entered his palace and boasted in it, he despised the words of Moses and did not set his heart to this as well. For otherwise, why was it necessary to inform us ״ויבא אל ביתו״? Rather, it came to inform us that he would boast in his palace, in the manner of Nebuchadnezzar, who said (Daniel 4:27): ״הלא דא היא בבל רבתא די אנא בניתה לבית מלכו בתקף חסני וליקר הדרי״, and it is written (Daniel 4:24): ״שליו הוית בביתי ורענן בהיכלי״. And just as the pride of Nebuchadnezzar was the cause of his downfall and ruin, so too was Pharaoh. Therefore all the warnings came from within his river and his palace — from them his punishment went forth, for the very places where he had boasted and sinned — from there all those plagues came upon him. Now the Holy One, blessed be He, informed him that He hardened his heart for two reasons: the first, so that His divinity be publicized by showing His signs and wonders in the world, both those that have passed and those yet to come — and therefore He used the expression שתי ('I have placed'), whose root מראה implies both past and future, which together total ten (י'), as if He said: ״למען שיתי״ — 'so that I may place — My ten signs. And therefore He said of them בקרבו ('in his midst'). The second, so that they recount to all generations the magnitude of His mighty acts and the power of His deeds."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:55.247685412Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}